     1	# ErgoWorks Consulting — Campaign Plan: how to review this
     2	
     3	**For: Marcus.** This folder is the full ErgoWorks Consulting corporate-campaign package. There are
     4	three ways in, fastest to deepest — pick by how much time you have.
     5	
     6	- **5 minutes** — the shareable web page: **https://marcuscaporaso.com/ergoworks/campaign-plan**
     7	  (private/unlisted; the directional story only). Good for a phone skim or to show the client.
     8	- **30 minutes** — read `1-strategy.pdf` (and `2-open-questions.pdf`) in the **`review-pack/`**
     9	  folder. The pack is split into one PDF per section so you can open just what you need:
    10	  `1-strategy` · `2-open-questions` · `E`, `B`, `A`, `D`, `C` appendices · plus `0-full-combined-pack.pdf`
    11	  (all of it in one file, with a clickable table of contents) if you'd rather scroll one document.
    12	  Each PDF is branded and print-ready. Start with `1-strategy`; open the appendices as needed.
    13	- **Deep dive** — the individual markdown files below, in this order.
    14	
    15	## What this is (one line)
    16	NOT a greenfield launch — ErgoWorks already runs a mature ~$40k/yr Google Search program. The plan =
    17	**optimise the existing Search + add the missing top-of-funnel (Meta demand-gen, a dedicated landing
    18	page, the free 15-minute Snapshot offer) + fix measurement.** Everything is planning only; nothing
    19	has been changed in any live account.
    20	
    21	## Reading order
    22	
    23	| # | File | What it is | Why you're reading it |
    24	|---|------|-----------|----------------------|
    25	| 1 | `ergoworks-campaign-plan.md` | **The strategy** (top layer) | The whole plan in one read: big idea, current state, offer, channels, measurement, 30/60/90, risks. Start here. |
    26	| 2 | `open-questions.md` | **The decisions only you can make** | The plan can't be built until these are answered. Read this even if you read nothing else. See "Your decisions" below. |
    27	| 3 | `appendices/E-existing-account-audit.md` | Existing-account diagnostic | What's actually happening in the live Search account today and what to fix first. |
    28	| 4 | `appendices/B-keyword-adgroup-sheets.md` | Media plan + build sheets | Budgets (3 scenarios), the keyword/ad-group build, negatives, and the new demand clusters we found. |
    29	| 5 | `appendices/A-landing-page-copy.md` | Landing-page wireframe + copy | The dedicated conversion page that replaces the generic WordPress destinations. |
    30	| 6 | `appendices/D-creative-briefs.md` | Offer, messaging & creative briefs | The offer ladder and the ad messaging for Search + Meta. |
    31	| 7 | `appendices/C-tracking-spec.md` | Measurement upgrade | How we make the numbers trustworthy (fix conversions, offline loop, privacy). Most technical — skim unless you want the detail. |
    32	
    33	Supporting evidence (not required reading, but it's what the plan is built on) lives in `research/`:
    34	the live account pulls, the Ahrefs keyword research, Lighthouse, and every review pass.
    35	
    36	## Your decisions (the short version of open-questions.md)
    37	These are the things that gate the build. The biggest few:
    38	
    39	1. **Budget** — is Meta funded on top of Search, or reallocated from it? What's the envelope?
    40	2. **Unit economics** — average contract value + delivery margin (sets what a lead is worth).
    41	3. **What do you actually issue after training** — an accredited certificate, a statement of
    42	   attainment, or attendance/completion records? (This drives a whole demand cluster and the LP copy.)
    43	4. **Service geography** — the live site says Sydney/Melbourne; converting demand spans further. What
    44	   coverage can you actually promise?
    45	5. **The Snapshot + offer ladder** — approved to go to market as described?
    46	6. **Meta access** — do we have the Business Manager / pixel / domain to build on?
    47	
    48	The rest (CRM, PMax fund-or-park, qualified-call rule, proof sign-off, who hosts the LP) are in
    49	`open-questions.md` with why each one matters.
    50	
    51	## Two things to know before you read
    52	- **Compliance is baked in, not bolted on.** No "we prevent injuries / guarantee WHS compliance"
    53	  claims anywhere — the plan promises controllable outputs only, and the Snapshot is always described
    54	  as preliminary guidance, not a compliance or medical assessment. These are AU legal guardrails.
    55	- **Numbers are honest about their limits.** Where we don't yet have clean data, the plan says so and
    56	  keeps the recommendation conditional rather than pretending certainty. That's deliberate.
     1	# ErgoWorks Consulting Campaign Plan
     2	
     3	## 1. Executive summary
     4	
     5	ErgoWorks Consulting already has a mature Google Search program. This plan is an optimisation and expansion programme—not a greenfield launch.
     6	
     7	The recommended strategy is to:
     8	
     9	1. **Protect and improve existing Search demand capture** by cleaning conversion goals, auditing query quality, strengthening Brand coverage and routing paid traffic to relevant landing-page variants.
    10	2. **Open Meta as the missing demand-generation lever** using workplace- and role-level creative, beginning with one consolidated prospecting ad set.
    11	3. **Launch a dedicated conversion landing page** centred on the free 15-minute Workplace Ergonomics Risk Snapshot—**preliminary guidance only—not a compliance assessment, compliance certification, medical assessment or diagnosis**.
    12	4. **Connect media to qualified pipeline outcomes** through CRM stages, offline conversion imports, server-side tagging and Meta CAPI.
    13	5. **Keep PMax and paid remarketing parked unless their funding, measurement, privacy and isolation gates can be met.**
    14	
    15	The preferred minimum launch envelope is **A$4,846 per 30-day planning period**: preserve the verified A$4,246 last-30-day Search baseline and add A$600 for one Meta prospecting ad set. This remains subject to commercial approval, Snapshot delivery capacity, landing-page readiness and the Australian privacy gate.
