   154	
   155	## 6. Identity, matching and hashing discipline
   156	
   157	### Permitted match parameters
   158	
   159	Aim to supply at least six applicable customer-information parameters to Meta CAPI for lead events, subject to consent and data availability:
   160	
   161	| Parameter | Collection rule | Transmission rule |
   162	|---|---|---|
   163	| `em` | Work email supplied in the enquiry | Lowercase, trim, then SHA-256 |
   164	| `ph` | Optional phone supplied for follow-up | Normalise to E.164, then SHA-256 |
   165	| `fn` | First name | Lowercase, trim, then SHA-256 |
   166	| `ln` | Last name, if collected | Lowercase, trim, then SHA-256 |
   167	| `ct` | Business/contact city, if legitimately collected | Lowercase, then SHA-256 |
   168	| `st` | State, if legitimately collected | Lowercase, then SHA-256 |
   169	| `zp` | Postcode, if legitimately collected | Lowercase, then SHA-256 |
   170	| `external_id` | Pseudonymous CRM contact or lead identifier | SHA-256 under one documented convention |
   171	| `fbp` | Meta browser identifier | Send un-hashed only where consent permits |
   172	| `fbc` | Meta click identifier derived from a valid click | Send un-hashed only where consent permits |
   173	| `client_ip_address` | Request metadata | CAPI transport only where approved; do not store longer than required |
   174	| `client_user_agent` | Request metadata | CAPI transport only where approved |
   175	| GCLID / GBRAID / WBRAID | Captured from auto-tagged Google visit | Store against the CRM lead and return through Google Data Manager |
   176	| `event_id` | Unique conversion identifier | Same un-hashed ID in browser and server copies for deduplication |
   177	
   178	Rules:
   179	
   180	- Hash PII before it leaves the controlled server environment.
   180	- Hash PII before it leaves the controlled server environment.
   181	- Never send plain email, phone, name or address data to advertising platforms.
   182	- Never double-hash values.
   183	- Use the same normalisation convention across web, CRM, Google and Meta.
   184	- Hashing is a matching control, not anonymisation and not permission to upload sensitive data.
   185	- Do not place PII in URLs, data-layer keys visible to third parties, GA4 parameters, campaign names or event names.
   186	
   187	## 7. Offline conversion loop
   188	
   189	### CRM capture at lead creation
   190	
   191	Each valid lead record should retain:
   192	
   193	- Unique `lead_id` and original `event_id`
   194	- Lead-entry event: Snapshot request, direct enquiry or call
   195	- Landing page and campaign metadata using sanitised values
   196	- GCLID, GBRAID, WBRAID, FBCLID-derived FBC, FBP and UTMs where consent permits
   197	- First-touch and latest-touch timestamps
   198	- Consulting service category
   199	- Call-tracking ID where relevant
   200	- Enquiry-processing consent state and timestamp
   201	- Marketing-consent state and timestamp as a separate field
   202	- CRM qualification, proposal and won timestamps
   203	- Approved monetary value basis
   204	- Upload status, upload timestamp and platform response
   205	
   206	### Google Data Manager
   207	
   208	- Configure Enhanced Conversions for Leads through Google Data Manager.
   209	- Upload `qualified_call`, `lead_qualified`, `proposal_sent` and `closed_won`.
   210	- Prefer upload latency under **24 hours**; **24–72 hours** is a warning state; more than **72 hours** fails the assigned methodology.
   211	- Preserve the original click identifier and event time.
   212	- Use a stable lead/order identifier to prevent duplicate imports.
   213	- Keep GA4 equivalents observational where a native Google conversion action is used.
   214	- Reconcile imported counts to CRM counts daily during launch and weekly thereafter.
   215	
   216	### Meta CAPI
   217	
   218	- Prefer CAPI Gateway for lower-maintenance server integration and matching support.
   219	- Send website lead events through Pixel and CAPI using identical `event_name` and `event_id`.
   220	- Send CRM stages through CAPI with an appropriate `action_source`; use `website` when the event is a continuation of a website-originated lead, and document any different source explicitly.
   221	- Include `event_time`, `event_source_url` only after sanitisation, customer-information parameters, value and `currency=AUD`.
   222	- Upload qualified and won changes within 24 hours where operationally possible.
   223	- Suppress uploads when the required consent or legal basis is absent.
   224	- Do not use the discontinued Meta Offline Conversions API workflow.
   225	
   226	## 8. Server-side implementation and quality gates
   227	
   228	Deploy sGTM on the first-party custom domain:
   229	
   230	`tags.ergoworksconsulting.com.au`
   231	
   232	Required controls:
   233	
   234	- Web GTM forwards eligible GA4 and advertising events to sGTM; avoid an undocumented direct-to-platform fallback.
   235	- Conversion Linker preserves GCLID, GBRAID and WBRAID.
   236	- The server container validates hostname, event name, schema version, consent state and allowed parameters.
   237	- Privacy transformations strip unapproved fields and sensitive query parameters.
   238	- IP address and user-agent are forwarded only where required, consented and approved.
   239	- Server logs retain only the minimum operational information and follow the approved retention schedule.
   240	- Meta Pixel and CAPI use matching event IDs and timestamps within five minutes for duplicated browser/server events.
   241	
   242	### Acceptance thresholds
   243	
   244	| Measure | Pass | Warning | Fail / launch blocker |
   245	|---|---:|---:|---:|
   246	| sGTM custom domain | Active | Configured, not active | Not configured |
   247	| CAPI implementation | CAPI Gateway active | Manual CAPI | Pixel only |
   248	| Meta EMQ for lead events | **≥8.0** | 6.0–7.9 | <6.0 |
   249	| Browser/server deduplication | **≥90%** | 70–89% | <70% |
   250	| Server/client hit ratio | **80–120%** | 50–79% or >120% pending duplicate diagnosis | <50% |
   251	| Match-parameter completeness | **6+ applicable parameters** | 4–5 | <4 |
   252	| Hash convention | Documented and verified | Documented, unverified | Inconsistent |
   253	| Offline import latency | **<24h** | 24–72h | >72h |
   254	| End-to-end validation | All canonical launch events pass | Partial coverage | A Primary event fails |
   255	
   256	A server/client ratio over 100% can be legitimate when server capture recovers browser-blocked events, but the required operating band is 80–120%. Ratios above 120% require duplicate, retry and trigger inspection before optimisation.
   257	
   258	## 9. Consent and Australian privacy controls
   259	
   260	### Consent sequence
   261	
   262	1. The CMP loads first and establishes default consent states.
   263	2. Apply **Consent Mode v2, default denied, with no client- or server-side analytics/advertising transmission before consent**, including denied defaults for:
   264	   - `analytics_storage`
   265	   - `ad_storage`
   266	   - `ad_user_data`
   267	   - `ad_personalization`
   268	3. No analytics, advertising, Pixel, CAPI, Enhanced Conversions, audience or call-tracking tag may transmit data before the required consent is recorded.
   269	4. Consent updates propagate to both web GTM and sGTM.
   270	5. The server container rejects advertising events that lack the required consent evidence.
   271	6. Withdrawal of consent stops future processing and triggers the approved deletion/suppression workflow.
   272	
   273	The assigned attribution methodology prefers an advanced-mode implementation. That conflicts with this campaign’s stricter requirement: **Consent Mode v2, default denied, with no client- or server-side analytics/advertising transmission before consent.** The stricter ErgoWorks rule governs. Cookieless pings may be considered only after explicit Australian legal/privacy approval. The methodology’s 700+ ad-clicks/day per country/domain modelling threshold is also not met by the verified last-30d Search volume of 407 clicks, so behavioural-modelling recovery must not be assumed.
   274	
   275	### Separate consent purposes
   276	
   277	- **Enquiry-processing consent:** permits Ergoworks to respond to the specific Snapshot request, direct enquiry or call. It must not be used as blanket marketing permission.
   278	- **Marketing consent:** optional, unchecked and separately worded for newsletters, nurture email, SMS or audience use where applicable.
   279	- Provide a clear privacy notice, platform disclosure, call-tracking disclosure, withdrawal mechanism and retention statement.
   280	
   280	
   281	No ad form may request injury history, symptoms, diagnosis, treatment, workers’ compensation details, claim status or other health information.
   282	
   283	## 10. Platform privacy allowlist and deny list
   284	
   285	### Allowlist: data that may be sent
   286	
   287	Only the following may be transmitted, after the applicable consent and privacy approval:
   288	
   289	**Events**
   290	
   291	- `view_service`
   292	- `form_start`
   293	- `snapshot_request`
   294	- `direct_enquiry`
   295	- `call_connected` without call content
   296	- `qualified_call`
   297	- `lead_qualified`
   298	- `proposal_sent`
   299	- `closed_won`
   300	- `newsletter_signup` only where separate marketing consent exists
   301	
   302	**Identifiers and matching data**
   303	
   304	- SHA-256-normalised `em`, `ph`, `fn`, `ln`, `ct`, `st`, `zp` and `external_id`
   305	- GCLID, GBRAID, WBRAID, FBP and FBC
   306	- Unique `event_id`, `lead_id`, `call_id`, proposal ID and deal ID where pseudonymous and approved
   307	- Client IP address and user agent only for the approved server-to-platform matching purpose
   308	- Consent state and timestamp
   309	- Sanitised campaign, source, medium and content identifiers
   310	
   311	**Non-sensitive business parameters**
   312	
   313	- Consulting service category
   314	- Offer type
   315	- Team-size band
   316	- Timeframe band
   317	- Qualification status using controlled values
   318	- Call duration and non-sensitive disposition code
   319	- Event timestamp
   320	- Approved conversion value and `AUD` currency
   321	- Schema version
   322	- Sanitised page path without query strings or personal data
   323	
   324	### Deny list: data that must never be sent
   325	
   326	- Injury descriptions or history
   327	- Pain, symptoms, diagnosis, disability or treatment information
   328	- Workers’ compensation, insurance or claim details
   329	- Medical or Physiotherapy appointment information
   330	- Return-to-work case notes or employee health records
   331	- Free-text enquiry content
   332	- Call recordings, transcripts or summaries
   333	- Names or details of affected employees
   334	- Health, injury or claim classifications inferred from page or form behaviour
   335	- Raw email, phone, name or postal address
   336	- Sensitive URL parameters, query strings, fragments, form values or page titles
   337	- Physiotherapy, occupational-health, ErgoEquip or Biosymm identifiers/events in the Consulting dataset
   338	- Passwords, authentication tokens, financial-account information or government identifiers
   339	- Any sensitive field merely transformed with SHA-256
   340	
   341	Hashing does not make health, injury, claim or clinical data acceptable for platform transmission.
   342	
   343	### Mandatory launch gate
   344	
   345	Before any Pixel, CAPI, Enhanced Conversions, CRM audience or offline-upload workflow is activated, Australian legal/privacy review must approve:
   346	
   347	- The complete CRM-to-platform field mapping
   348	- Consent wording and CMP behaviour
   349	- Enquiry versus marketing-consent separation
   350	- Each permitted event and identifier
   351	- Call tracking and any recording practices
   352	- Data retention and deletion periods
   353	- Server log contents and hosting/data-residency arrangements
   354	- Google and Meta upload purposes
   355	- Privacy-policy and collection-notice updates
   356	
   357	Failure to obtain this approval blocks launch.
   358	
   359	## 11. Attribution configuration
   360	
   361	- Use **Data-Driven Attribution (DDA)** for eligible Google Ads conversion actions and GA4 reporting.
   362	- Do not use first-click, linear, position-based, time-decay or other rule-based attribution.
   363	- Use a provisional **30-day Google click window** for B2B lead generation, then review actual conversion lag quarterly. Extend toward 60–90 days only if CRM evidence shows the sales cycle requires it.
   364	- Treat Meta’s platform attribution using its supported settings; begin with the standard 7-day click/1-day view reporting context where required, but treat view-through conversions as directional rather than proof of incrementality.
   365	- Use CRM-qualified and closed-won records as the commercial source of truth across platforms.
   366	- Report platform-attributed, GA4-attributed and CRM-sourced outcomes separately; do not force them to reconcile as if they use identical windows and models.
   367	- Do not introduce rule-based attribution or Video Action Campaign recommendations.
   368	
   369	## 12. Call-tracking implementation
   370	
   371	Select an Australian-compatible call-tracking provider with:
   372	
   373	- Dynamic number insertion
   374	- Google Ads and GA4 integration
   375	- GCLID/GBRAID/WBRAID capture
   376	- Meta/UTM attribution
   377	- CRM integration or webhook support
   378	- Duration and controlled disposition fields
   379	- Duplicate-caller recognition
   380	- Consent and privacy controls
   381	- Configurable recording with recording disabled unless separately approved
   382	
   383	Implementation rules:
   384	
   385	- Maintain one stable business number as the fallback.
   386	- Apply dynamic number replacement only on approved ErgoWorks Consulting pages.
   387	- Do not replace numbers on Physiotherapy, ErgoEquip or Biosymm properties.
   388	- Create `call_connected` when the call connects.
   389	- Create `qualified_call` only after the W-Audit qualification rule is satisfied.
   390	- Do not infer qualification solely from duration.
   391	- Send no call content, clinical details or free-text notes to an advertising platform.
   392	- Reconcile call-platform totals to CRM dispositions and Google call reporting weekly.
   393	
   394	## 13. KPI and reconciliation framework
   395	
   396	The primary commercial optimisation KPI is **cost per qualified lead**, not reported platform conversions or raw form CPL.
   397	
   398	| KPI | Formula | Use |
   399	|---|---|---|
   400	| Raw CPL | Media spend ÷ valid Snapshot requests, direct enquiries and connected lead calls | Entry-stage efficiency |
   401	| Cost per qualified lead | Media spend ÷ unique CRM-qualified leads | Primary channel KPI |
   402	| Qualified-lead rate | Qualified leads ÷ valid raw leads | Lead-quality diagnostic |
   403	| Cost per proposal | Media spend ÷ proposals sent | Pipeline efficiency |
   404	| CAC | Total attributable acquisition cost ÷ closed-won customers | Commercial efficiency |
   405	| Media-only CAC | Media spend ÷ closed-won customers | Channel comparison; label clearly |
   406	| Lead-to-won rate | Closed-won customers ÷ valid raw leads | Funnel calibration |
   407	| Qualified-lead-to-won rate | Closed-won customers ÷ qualified leads | Target-CPL calibration |
   408	| Target qualified CPL | Target CAC × qualified-lead-to-won rate | Maximum affordable qualified-lead cost |
   409	| Target raw CPL | Target qualified CPL × qualified-lead rate | Maximum affordable raw-lead cost |
   410	| Pipeline value per qualified lead | Expected contribution profit × qualified-lead-to-won probability | Offline conversion value |
   411	| Platform-to-CRM discrepancy | Absolute platform conversions minus matched CRM conversions, divided by CRM conversions | Tracking diagnostic |
   412	| Missing-click-ID rate | Leads without eligible click ID ÷ attributable paid leads | Offline-match diagnostic |
   413	| Duplicate-lead rate | Duplicate lead records ÷ total lead records | CRM integrity |
   414	| Upload success rate | Accepted offline events ÷ eligible events | Pipeline health |
   415	
   416	“Total attributable acquisition cost” should include media, agency/consulting fees, creative, landing-page costs and other agreed acquisition costs. Contribution profit, not headline revenue, should be used for target-CAC decisions where available.
   417	
   418	No target CAC, target qualified CPL or scale threshold should be committed until average contract value, contribution margin, qualification rate and close rate are verified.
   419	
   420	### First 90-day measurement commitments
   421	
   422	Commit only to measurable leading indicators:
   423	
   424	- 100% of approved Primary actions documented and tested
   425	- Newsletter and soft actions removed from Consulting bidding goals
   426	- Physiotherapy actions excluded from campaign 700072710 goals
   427	- CRM lead IDs and event IDs present on valid lead records
   428	- Offline uploads operating within the latency thresholds
   429	- Meta lead-event EMQ at least 8.0
   430	- Browser/server deduplication at least 90%
   431	- Server/client hit ratio between 80% and 120%
   432	- At least six applicable Meta match parameters on eligible lead events
   433	- Platform-to-CRM reconciliation reported weekly
   434	- Zero sensitive-data events or parameters detected in launch testing
   435	
   436	These are implementation and data-quality commitments, not promised reductions in CPL, claims or injury outcomes.
   437	
   438	## 14. Validation and launch checklist
   439	
   440	- [ ] Campaign-specific Google conversion goals documented
   441	- [ ] Newsletter, email clicks and raw call actions made Secondary for Consulting
   442	- [ ] Physiotherapy actions excluded from campaign 700072710
   443	- [ ] Consulting-only GA4 stream or equivalent enforced isolation verified
   444	- [ ] Dedicated Meta Pixel/Dataset created for ErgoWorks Consulting
   445	- [ ] `tags.ergoworksconsulting.com.au` active
   446	- [ ] Conversion Linker tested with GCLID, GBRAID and WBRAID
   447	- [ ] CMP blocks all unconsented client and server transmissions
   448	- [ ] Enquiry and marketing consent stored separately
   449	- [ ] Snapshot caveat shown beside every Snapshot conversion point
   450	- [ ] Form schema contains no health, injury or claim fields
   451	- [ ] Server allowlist and deny list enforced
   452	- [ ] Pixel and CAPI event names and IDs match
   453	- [ ] Deduplication, EMQ and server/client ratios pass
   454	- [ ] Google Data Manager qualified/won test imports accepted
   455	- [ ] Meta CAPI qualified/won test events accepted
   456	- [ ] Call rule matches Appendix E/W-Audit
   457	- [ ] No tags fire on Physiotherapy, ErgoEquip or Biosymm properties
   458	- [ ] DDA and conversion windows verified
   459	- [ ] CRM reconciliation report approved
   460	- [ ] Australian legal/privacy approval recorded

